{
  "abstract": "We study source residues of Collatz parity words conditioned to keep every earlier multiplicative coefficient above one. We prove effective superpolynomial decay of the average absolute Fourier coefficient conditional on the full four-bit count skeleton, uniformly in the remaining survival horizon and in every prescribed subexponential ordinary-numerator window. More precisely, for conductor exponent $m$ and a budget $B\\ge\\max\\{(\\log m)^4,\\log|b|+5\\}$ with $B\\le e^{-64}m$, the bound is $C\\exp[-c\\log m\\,\\log(m/B)/\\log(2+\\log(m/B))]$. In the window $|b|\\le e^{\\sqrt m}$ this becomes $C\\exp[-c(\\log m)^2/\\log\\log m]$. Two mechanisms yield the improvement. A fixed height band below an original arithmetic triangle consists of actual good phases until its count top is crossed, even after side exits. Soft killing in this band and reversal control late crossings uniformly in entrance height; the established ternary-seed estimate controls early crossings. The resulting complete-clock kernel gives a nonnegative supermartingale for accumulated gap payment. A potential of the running count then converts a classical valuation bound into sufficient payment, explicitly accounting for the final unfinished episode. The rare-excursion normalization is paid once. Conditional interval discrepancy gives actual stopping counts and full-skeleton comparison for delays of order $(\\log N)^2/\\log\\log N$; density interpolation transfers established Brownian laws and uncapped moments. The proof imports specified clock, killed-walk and classical valuation results. Finite CPU checks support the arithmetic and random-walk interfaces. Effective thresholds are conservative. Fixed-ratio delay and descent of individual integers remain open.",
